  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Kunduru Manikanta vs The State Of Andhra Pradesh on 25 January, 2021 2 3 In The High Court Of Andhra Pradesh At Amaravati",
    "facts": "The petitioners before this Court are Al and A2 in the above said crime. 3. The brief facts of the case are that while the Police were conducting vehicle search two persons came on Hero Splendor Plus Vehicle bearing No.AP 39 DL 7074 from Mallela village and on seeing the Police they tried to escape. But the Police apprehended them and on questioning they revealed that they have purchased the said products from an unknown lorry driver for the purpose of selling the same at higher prices. On verification the Police found Blue Bull and other varieties of tobacco products. Basing on the same the present crime is registered against the petitioners and tobacco products were seized.",
    "arguments": "Learned counsel for the petitioners submits that Sections 270 and 272 I.P.C. relate to food safety standards. Section 270 LPC. speaks about malignant act likely to spread infection of disease dangerous to life and Section 273 of I.P.C. speaks about sale of Noxious Food or Drink. Neither of the sections is applicable to the present case as tobacco will not spread any infection and it does not fall under the category of food or drink or poison. Moreover, the Police have no power to register the offences under above Sections except the designated officers like Food Safety Officer. 6, Learned Counsel for the petitioners placed reliance on the common order dated 18.12.2019 passed by a coordinate Bench of this Court in Criminal Petition Nos.5421 of 2019 and batch, wherein it was held that tobacco does not fall within the definition of \"food\" as specified under Section 3(i)(j) of the FSS Act.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "Learned counsel for the petitioners submits that Sections 270 and 272 I.P.C. relate to food safety standards. Section 270 LPC. speaks about malignant act likely to spread infection of disease dangerous to life and Section 273 of I.P.C. speaks about sale of Noxious Food or Drink. Neither of the sections is applicable to the present case as tobacco will not spread any infection and it does not fall under the category of food or drink or poison. Moreover, the Police have no power to register the offences under above Sections except the designated officers like Food Safety Officer. 6, Learned Counsel for the petitioners placed reliance on the common order dated 18.12.2019 passed by a coordinate Bench of this Court in Criminal Petition Nos.5421 of 2019 and batch, wherein it was held that tobacco does not fall within the definition of \"food\" as specified under Section 3(i)(j) of the FSS Act.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": ""
  },
